[THOUGHT]  
This 5-year-old male is asymptomatic but exhibits classic, objective signs of hemodynamically significant coarctation of the aorta (COA):  
- Severe hypertension for age (150/80 mm Hg — >40 mm Hg above 95th percentile systolic), with upper-lower extremity BP gradient (higher in arms, lower in legs);  
- Discrepant peripheral pulses (2+ radial vs. trace femoral), indicating impaired distal aortic perfusion;  
- Characteristic auscultatory findings: systolic ejection click (suggesting bicuspid aortic valve, present in ~50–85% of COA cases) and a 2/6 long systolic murmur radiating to the back — consistent with turbulent flow across the coarctation site;  
- Definitive echocardiographic confirmation of discrete postductal coarctation (distal to left subclavian artery) with measurable transcoarctation gradient.  

All findings converge robustly on COA as the unifying diagnosis. No alternative explanation accounts for the triad of upper-body hypertension, weak lower-extremity pulses, and back-radiating systolic murmur with ejection click. Secondary causes of childhood hypertension (e.g., renal artery stenosis, pheochromocytoma, Cushing syndrome) are unsupported by history, exam, or imaging — and would not produce the pulse disparity or murmur morphology seen here. The echocardiogram rules out other structural lesions (e.g., severe AS, PDA, or arch interruption) as primary drivers.

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Coarctation of the Aorta

